course: world
lesson: 43
title: Mountains, rivers and seas, and how the land is shaped
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The outer shell of the Earth is not one piece: it is broken into huge slabs called plates, which float on hot soft rock and move a few centimetres a year, about as fast as your fingernails grow.
explain: Where two plates push together, the rock crumples and rises up as mountains over millions of years; the Himalayas, with Mount Everest, are still growing because India keeps pushing into Asia.
explain: Where plates grind or slip past each other, the ground shakes in an earthquake; where melted rock called magma finds a way up through cracks, it bursts out as lava from a volcano.
explain: Water shapes the land too: a river carries sand and stones that scrape its bed, and over thousands of years it cuts a deep valley shaped like the letter V; where it slows down near the sea, it drops its sand and mud and builds a delta.
explain: A lake is water resting in a hollow of the land, a sea is a part of the ocean partly closed by land, and a river is water flowing downhill in a channel from its source to its mouth.
fact: The Earth's outer shell is broken into huge plates that move very slowly.
fact: Mountains rise where plates push against each other for millions of years.
fact: Mount Everest in the Himalayas is the highest mountain on Earth, about 8,849 metres high.
fact: An earthquake is a sudden shaking of the ground when rocks along a crack slip.
fact: A volcano is an opening where melted rock, ash and gas come out from deep inside the Earth.
fact: Melted rock is called magma under the ground and lava when it comes out on the surface.
fact: A river starts at its source and ends at its mouth, where it flows into a sea, a lake or another river.
fact: Over thousands of years a river cuts a valley into the land with the sand and stones it carries.
fact: A delta is flat land built from the mud and sand a river drops where it slows down at its mouth.
fact: A lake is a body of water lying in a hollow of the land, and most lakes have fresh water.
fact: A sea is a part of the ocean that is partly surrounded by land.
fact: Wind, rain, ice and frost slowly break rocks into smaller pieces; this is called weathering.
fact: Water grows bigger when it freezes, so ice in a crack pushes the rock apart.
fact: Most earthquakes and volcanoes happen where plates meet.
fact: Some rock high in mountains formed long ago under the sea and was pushed up when plates collided, so it can hold shells of sea animals.
exercise: What is the highest mountain on Earth? | Mount Everest | Mount Kosciuszko | Mont Blanc | Mount Kilimanjaro
exercise: How are most high mountains made? | Plates of the Earth's shell push together and crumple the rock | Wind piles up sand into huge heaps over many years | People and animals carry stones into big piles | Rain washes soil from the valleys onto the hills
exercise: What happens during an earthquake? | Rocks slip and the ground shakes | Wind blows so hard that houses shake | A volcano sinks back into the ground | Thunder in the clouds shakes the earth
exercise: What comes out of an erupting volcano? | Lava, ash and gas from deep inside the Earth | Hot water from a lake under the mountain | Burning trees from the forest on its slopes | Sand and dust blown there by strong desert winds
exercise: What is magma called when it comes out on the surface? | Lava | Ash | Granite | Steam
exercise: What is the source of a river? | The place where the river begins | The place where it flows into the sea | The widest part of the river | A bridge across the river
exercise: What is the mouth of a river? | Where it flows into a sea, lake or river | The place high in the hills where it begins | The deepest and darkest pool along the river | The sharp bend where the river turns around
exercise: How does a river make a valley? | The sand and stones it carries slowly scrape away the land | It pushes the mountains apart with its strong, fast current | It melts the rocks with its cold water | The fish in it dig the ground away
exercise: Why does a river build a delta at its mouth? | It slows down and drops the mud and sand it carried | The sea throws stones onto the shore during big winter storms | Farmers build up the land to grow crops | The river freezes and its ice turns into land
exercise: How is a sea different from a lake? | A sea is part of the ocean and a lake lies in a hollow of the land | A sea is always smaller than a lake | A lake is always salty and a sea is always fresh | A sea has no waves and a lake has big waves
exercise: Why do the plates of the Earth move? | They ride on hot soft rock that slowly flows | The Moon pulls them around a little every month | The oceans push them along with their big waves | Earthquakes keep pushing them away from each other
exercise: About how fast do the Earth's plates move? | A few centimetres a year | A few metres every single day | A few kilometres a year | They do not move at all
exercise: What is weathering? | The slow breaking of rocks by wind, rain, ice and frost | A sudden change of weather from bright sunshine to heavy rain | The growth of rocks bigger and bigger over time | The melting of rocks inside a volcano
exercise: Why can water freezing in a crack split a rock? | Water grows bigger when it freezes and pushes the crack open | Ice is heavier than rock and crushes it | Frost makes the rock soft like clay | Cold air makes the rock shrink to nothing
test: Climbers find shells of sea animals in rocks near the top of a high mountain. How can that be? | The rock formed under the sea and was pushed up as plates collided | Birds carried the shells up the mountain one by one | Climbers dropped the shells there long ago | The sea once reached as high as the mountain top
test: Which change in the land happens fastest? | An earthquake shaking the ground | A river cutting a deep valley | Plates pushing up a mountain range | Rain wearing away a hill
test: A river is very muddy where it enters the sea, and the land there is flat and grows bigger every year. What is forming? | A delta | A volcano | A mountain range | A glacier
test: Which is true about lava and magma? | They are the same melted rock, above and below the ground | Lava is cold rock and magma is hot rock | Magma comes down from the Sun and lava comes from the Earth | Lava is water and magma is melted ice
test: Why are earthquakes and volcanoes common in the same places? | Both happen mostly where plates meet | Both happen only in very cold countries | Both happen only near big rivers and lakes | Both are caused by very strong winds blowing
test: A deep valley has steep sides like the letter V and a stream at the bottom. What made it? | The stream, wearing the rock away over thousands of years | An earthquake that split the ground wide open in a single day | People who dug it to make a road | A volcano that blew the rock away
test: A rock left in a sunny, frosty place cracks into pieces after many winters. What has happened? | Weathering by freezing water in its cracks | An earthquake under the rock | Lava pouring over the rock | The rock dried out and turned into sand at once
test: Why is Mount Everest still very slowly getting higher? | India is still pushing into Asia and lifting the rock | Snow keeps falling and piling up higher and higher on top of it | Climbers bring stones to the top every year | The volcano inside it keeps erupting
test: Where does a river finish its journey? | At its mouth | At its source, high in the mountains | At the deepest point of its valley | Where the first bridge crosses it
test: how'd a river make a whole valley, it's just water | Over thousands of years the sand and stones it carries cut the land | It floods once and digs it out | The valley was there and the river found it | Fish dig the valley as they swim
test: lava n magma whats the diff | Magma is melted rock underground, lava is the same rock on the surface | Lava is hotter rock than magma | Magma is ash and lava is gas | They are two different kinds of mountain
test: my brother says mountains just popped up quickly ages ago. true? | No, they rise slowly as plates push together for millions of years | Yes, they popped up in one day | Yes, volcanoes made all of them | No, they are slowly sinking into the sea
test: Ava says most lakes are salty like the sea. right? | No, most lakes have fresh water | Yes, all lakes are salty | Yes, lakes are pieces of the sea | No, lakes have no water in winter
exam: Identify the principal cause of the formation of mountains. | The pressure of the Earth's plates against one another | The deposit of sand by rivers | The erosion of valleys by wind | The accumulation of snow upon the summits over many centuries
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately distinguishes magma from lava? | Magma lies beneath the ground; lava has emerged upon the surface | Magma has emerged upon the surface; lava lies beneath the ground | Magma is solid rock; lava is liquid rock | Magma is volcanic ash; lava is volcanic gas
exam: Which of the following is designated by the term delta? | Flat land formed from mud and sand deposited at a river's mouth | The source of a river among mountains | A deep valley carved by a glacier | A lake lying in a hollow of the land
exam: Identify the highest mountain upon the Earth. | Mount Everest | Mount Kilimanjaro | Mont Blanc | Mount Denali
